    \ingroup vscodeext-how-to-csharp

    \title Debug Qt Bridge for C# applications

    You can debug the QML code of a \l {Qt Bridge for C#} application with the
    QML debugger of \QVSC. To debug the C# code, use the debugger of the C# Dev
    Kit extension.

    Before you start debugging, build the application. For more information,
    see \l {vscodeext-how-to-build-csharp.html}
    {Build and run Qt Bridge for C# applications}.

    \section1 Debug QML code

    Projects that you create from the \uicontrol {Qt Bridge for C# application}
    template have a QML debugger configuration in the \c {.vscode/launch.json}
    file. To debug the QML code of the application, select the
    \uicontrol {Qt Bridge: QML} debug configuration, and start debugging.

    For other projects, add the configuration to the \c {launch.json} file of
    the project. For more information, see \l {Debug Qt applications}. The
    configuration uses \c {${command:qt-csharp.launchTargetPath}} as the
    program:

    \badcode
    "configurations": [
        {
            "name": "Qt Bridge: QML",
            "type": "qml",
            "request": "launch",
            "program": "${command:qt-csharp.launchTargetPath}"
        }
    ]
    \endcode

    The command resolves the executable of the project that contains the file
    in the editor, or asks you to select a project if there are several. It
    does not build the project. If you built the project in several
    configurations or for several target frameworks, select the build to use
    with \uicontrol {Qt Bridge C#: Select QML Build Metadata}.

    When the debugger stops, the editor shows the QML files of your project,
    not the copies that the build makes of them.

    \note Breakpoints in QML code that runs right when the application starts
    can be missed.

    For more information about debugging QML code, such as attaching to a
    running application, see \l {Debug Qt Quick applications}.

    \section1 Debug C# code

    To debug the C# code of the application, install the C# Dev Kit extension,
    and start the C# debugger from the \uicontrol {Solution Explorer} view:
    right-click the project, and select \uicontrol Debug. If the project has a
    QML debugger configuration, \key F5 starts the QML debugger instead.

    \sa {vscodeext-how-to-build-csharp.html}
        {Build and run Qt Bridge for C# applications}
*/
